1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,725
Aarav turns the dial of his meter.

2
00:00:02,725 --> 00:00:04,510
AV with a straight line.

3
00:00:04,510 --> 00:00:06,103
AV with a wavy line.

4
00:00:06,103 --> 00:00:08,809
Numbers like two, twenty and two hundred.

5
00:00:09,159 --> 00:00:11,019
What do all these markings mean?

6
00:00:11,519 --> 00:00:13,470
Every meter speaks in symbols.

7
00:00:13,470 --> 00:00:14,640
Let's learn them.

8
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:19,018
AV with a straight line and
a dashed line means DC volts.

9
00:00:19,018 --> 00:00:21,371
A steady voltage, like a battery.

10
00:00:21,721 --> 00:00:24,398
AV with a wavy line means AC volts.

11
00:00:24,398 --> 00:00:28,623
A voltage that keeps changing
direction, like the supply at home.

12
00:00:28,973 --> 00:00:31,230
A stands for amperes, the current.

13
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:34,639
The Greek letter omega stands
for ohms, the resistance.

14
00:00:34,989 --> 00:00:36,209
Now the sockets.

15
00:00:36,209 --> 00:00:40,999
The black lead always goes into the
socket marked COM, which means common.

16
00:00:41,349 --> 00:00:44,750
The red lead goes into the
socket for volts and ohms.

17
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:48,237
Large currents often get a
separate socket of their own.

18
00:00:48,587 --> 00:00:53,209
In circuit diagrams, a voltmeter is
a circle with a V, an ammeter is a

19
00:00:53,209 --> 00:00:57,214
circle with an A, and a
galvanometer is a circle with a G.

20
00:00:57,864 --> 00:00:58,923
Now, ranges.

21
00:00:58,923 --> 00:01:03,106
A range is the largest value the
meter can show on that setting.

22
00:01:03,456 --> 00:01:05,320
Here is a nine volt battery.

23
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:08,520
On the two volt range, the
meter cannot show nine.

24
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:11,673
The display says OL, which
means over the limit.

25
00:01:12,023 --> 00:01:14,229
Switch to the twenty volt range.

26
00:01:14,229 --> 00:01:16,595
Now it reads nine point zero zero.

27
00:01:16,945 --> 00:01:20,747
On the two hundred volt range,
it reads nine point zero.

28
00:01:20,747 --> 00:01:23,311
Same voltage, but one digit is lost.

29
00:01:23,311 --> 00:01:26,963
So pick the lowest range that
is still above the value.

30
00:01:27,613 --> 00:01:29,824
The oscilloscope has ranges too.

31
00:01:29,824 --> 00:01:33,002
Its volts per division
knob is a range switch.

32
00:01:33,352 --> 00:01:37,445
At one volt per division, the nine
volt line shoots off the top of the

33
00:01:37,445 --> 00:01:38,345
screen.

34
00:01:38,388 --> 00:01:41,075
Turn the knob to five volts per division.

35
00:01:41,075 --> 00:01:45,502
Now the line sits at one point eight
divisions, safely on the screen.

36
00:01:46,152 --> 00:01:48,101
Its other controls are simple.

37
00:01:48,101 --> 00:01:52,635
Intensity sets the brightness, focus
makes the trace sharp, and the position

38
00:01:52,635 --> 00:01:55,325
knobs move it up, down, left or right.

39
00:01:55,975 --> 00:01:58,664
An analogue meter has
a needle and a scale.

40
00:01:58,664 --> 00:02:00,274
Here is how to read it.

41
00:02:00,624 --> 00:02:04,457
First, note where the needle
stops, counted in scale divisions.

42
00:02:05,157 --> 00:02:07,895
Divide by the total
divisions on the scale.

43
00:02:07,895 --> 00:02:10,195
That is the fraction of full scale.

44
00:02:10,895 --> 00:02:12,472
Multiply by the range.

45
00:02:12,472 --> 00:02:13,998
That is your reading.

46
00:02:14,698 --> 00:02:18,864
The same needle position on a
bigger range means a bigger value.

47
00:02:18,864 --> 00:02:21,691
So always check the range before you read.

48
00:02:22,341 --> 00:02:23,458
Worked example.

49
00:02:23,458 --> 00:02:25,402
A scale has fifty divisions.

50
00:02:25,402 --> 00:02:29,100
On the ten volt range, the
needle stops at thirty two.

51
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:32,367
The reading is thirty two
over fifty, times ten.

52
00:02:32,967 --> 00:02:37,683
That is zero point six four times
ten, which is six point four volts.

53
00:02:38,333 --> 00:02:39,790
Now, the precautions.

54
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:41,040
One.

55
00:02:40,642 --> 00:02:43,573
Start on the highest
range, then step down.

56
00:02:43,573 --> 00:02:46,928
A big value on a small
range can damage the meter.

57
00:02:47,278 --> 00:02:48,178
Two.

58
00:02:47,786 --> 00:02:49,892
For DC, watch the polarity.

59
00:02:49,892 --> 00:02:51,997
Red to plus, black to minus.

60
00:02:51,997 --> 00:02:55,689
Reverse it, and an analogue
needle kicks the wrong way.

61
00:02:56,039 --> 00:02:56,939
Three.

62
00:02:56,627 --> 00:02:59,346
Measure resistance only
with the power off.

63
00:02:59,346 --> 00:03:03,560
For an analogue ohmmeter, touch the
probes together and set the needle

64
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:04,649
to zero first.

65
00:03:04,999 --> 00:03:05,899
Four.

66
00:03:05,506 --> 00:03:08,772
Read the needle with your
eye straight in front of it.

67
00:03:08,772 --> 00:03:11,468
Looking from the side
gives a parallax error.

68
00:03:11,818 --> 00:03:12,718
Five.

69
00:03:12,367 --> 00:03:17,273
When you finish, turn the dial to off,
so the cell inside does not drain.

70
00:03:17,923 --> 00:03:19,460
Highest range first.

71
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:20,727
Then step down.

72
00:03:21,527 --> 00:03:22,993
Now, the exam trap.

73
00:03:23,343 --> 00:03:27,628
Many students think OL on the
display means the meter is broken.

74
00:03:27,628 --> 00:03:28,659
It does not.

75
00:03:29,009 --> 00:03:31,565
OL means the value is over the range.

76
00:03:31,565 --> 00:03:33,275
Switch to a higher range.

77
00:03:33,275 --> 00:03:37,636
On the ohms setting, it means the
resistance is too high to show, like

78
00:03:37,636 --> 00:03:38,804
an open circuit.

79
00:03:39,454 --> 00:03:40,906
Where is this asked?

80
00:03:40,906 --> 00:03:42,358
In PUC board papers.

81
00:03:42,358 --> 00:03:46,976
Name the symbols, list the precautions,
and read a value from a scale and

82
00:03:46,976 --> 00:03:47,876
a range.

83
00:03:48,377 --> 00:03:49,277
Try this.

84
00:03:49,172 --> 00:03:52,254
An analogue voltmeter has
a fifty division scale.

85
00:03:52,254 --> 00:03:55,899
On the ten volt range, the
needle stops at thirty five.

86
00:03:55,899 --> 00:03:59,244
The meter is switched to
the twenty five volt range.

87
00:03:59,244 --> 00:04:01,338
Where will the needle stop now?

88
00:04:06,938 --> 00:04:07,838
C.

89
00:04:07,448 --> 00:04:09,089
Fourteen divisions.

90
00:04:09,439 --> 00:04:11,207
First, find the voltage.

91
00:04:11,207 --> 00:04:14,656
Thirty five over fifty,
times ten, is seven volts.

92
00:04:15,006 --> 00:04:18,450
The voltage does not change
when you change the range.

93
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,384
On the twenty five volt range, seven
over twenty five, times fifty, is

94
00:04:23,384 --> 00:04:24,691
fourteen divisions.

95
00:04:25,341 --> 00:04:26,862
Screenshot this card.

96
00:04:30,062 --> 00:04:32,425
Next, medical electronic instruments.

97
00:04:32,425 --> 00:04:36,727
How does a small clip on your finger
read the oxygen in your blood?
